  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness based on whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes. Lottery B has a higher probability (25%) on the favorable outcome than Lottery A (20%), so B is more left-skewed. The reason would thus prefer B. However, the individual’s deliberation does not mention skewness or the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. Instead, they focus on the magnitude of the gain and the small difference in probability (5 percentage points), and they emphasize the absence of loss. They are willing to accept a lower probability for a much higher reward, indicating a focus on expected value or gain magnitude rather than the shape of the distribution. There is no mention of probability concentration on favorable outcomes or a comparison of skewness. Therefore, the reasoning process described does not align with the skewness-based rule. The individual did not use the skewness reason to make the decision.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
